n general contributed significantly to the weakening of the Eastern Han Dynasty's central authority in 184 CE, hastening its collapse."}, {"sourceSlug": "bo-cai", "sourceName": "Bo Cai", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "zhang-jiao", "targetName": "Zhang Jiao", "context": "Bo Cai acted as a leading general under the spiritual and military command of Zhang Jiao, the founder of the Way of Great Peace, during the 184 CE rebellion."}], "places": [{"name": "Changshe, China", "role": "Major battle site"}, {"name": "Yingchuan Commandery, China", "role": "Region of early victories"}, {"name": "Luoyang, China", "role": "Imperial capital threatened"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Bo Cai", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bo-cai"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q887350", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bo_Cai_(rebel)"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bo Cai's leadership played a critical role in the Yellow Turban Rebellion, a massive uprising that severely weakened the Eastern Han Dynasty and directly contributed to its eventual fragmentation into the Three Kingdoms period, impacting millions of lives and centuries of Chinese history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-21T19:21:36Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44939190700", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Bo Cai (b. 200 CE) was a public servant or civil administrator of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of military leaders & commanders.", "newValue": "Bo Cai was a pivotal military commander of the Yellow Turban Rebellion, a cataclysmic peasant uprising that erupted across the Eastern Han Dynasty in 184 CE. Operating primarily in the central plains of China, his campaigns were instrumental in challenging the crumbling imperial authority and accelerating the dynasty's descent into fragmentation. Though his birth year is unrecorded, his death in battle in 184 CE marks him as a figure whose brief but intense period of activity profoundly impacted the course of Chinese history, embodying the widespread discontent that characterized the late Classical era in East Asia.

His most notable actions include leading a substantial rebel force that initially routed the imperial general Zhu Jun, forcing him to retreat. Bo Cai subsequently laid siege to Changshe (modern-day Changge, Henan province), a crucial strategic point. This siege lasted for months, putting immense pressure on the Han government. However, his forces were ultimately defeated by the combined strategies of Huangfu Song and Zhu Jun, who exploited a strong wind to launch a devastating fire attack on the Yellow Turban encampment, scattering Bo Cai's army and marking a significant turning point in the rebellion's initial phase.

While Bo Cai's direct military efforts were ultimately suppressed, his initial successes and the sheer scale of the Yellow Turban Rebellion he helped lead inflicted irreparable damage upon the Eastern Han state. The rebellion necessitated the mobilization of regional armies and the empowerment of local strongmen, who, after quelling the uprising, refused to relinquish their newfound autonomy. This directly paved the way for the warlordism that dominated the following decades and culminated in the Three Kingdoms period, fundamentally altering China's political landscape for centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-21T19:21:36Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44939190700", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "200 CE"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 CE"}], "newValue": ["Widespread corruption and increasing eunuch influence in the Eastern Han court eroded central authority and public trust.", "Severe economic hardship, landlessness, and frequent natural disasters fueled peasant discontent and desperation across Han China.", "The rise of millenarian Taoist movements, particularly the Way of Great Peace led by Zhang Jiao, provided an ideological framework for rebellion."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-21T19:21:36Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44939190700", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "184 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "184 CE"}], "newValue": ["The Yellow Turban Rebellion, significantly aided by Bo Cai's command, profoundly destabilized the Eastern Han Dynasty, accelerating its decline.", "The necessity of suppressing the rebellion empo